  "testable_hypotheses_or_predictions": [
    "Interior environments furnished with appearance wood products will lead to higher self-reported psychological well-being and positive emotional states compared to environments with synthetic materials.",
    "Exposure to wood-dominated interior spaces will result in lower physiological stress markers (e.g., cortisol, heart rate variability) compared to exposure to spaces dominated by artificial materials.",
    "Individuals will exhibit a stronger aesthetic preference for interior spaces incorporating natural wood materials compared to those with predominantly artificial materials.",
    "The inclusion of psychological benefits (e.g., stress reduction, connection to nature) in the marketing of appearance wood products will increase consumer preference and purchase intent."
  ],
  "bridge_warrants": [
    "The paper connects the 'healthy homes' framework to 'psychological well-being' by proposing its addition as a crucial component.",
    "It links environmental psychology principles (e.g., preference for nature) to architectural design and material selection (wood) for enhancing human well-being.",
    "It bridges the concept of natural material preference (specifically wood) with mechanisms of stress reduction and biophilia, suggesting wood can serve as a biophilic element indoors."
  ],
  "methodological_critiques": [],
  "model_structure": "The paper proposes an expansion of the existing 'Occupant Health' framework for healthy homes. This expanded model integrates 'Psychological Well-being' as a distinct component alongside existing considerations such as 'Indoor Air', 'Water', 'Lighting', and 'Acoustics' (as depicted in Fig. 1).",
